theorem.content:
\label{thm:main}
For a skew-symmetrizable matrix $B$, the following are equivalent:
\begin{itemize}
\item[$(1)$] $B$ is of finite type.
\item[$(2)$] The $g$-fan $\mathcal{F}(B)$ is complete, that is, its support $|\mathcal{F}(B)|=\mathbb{R}^n$.
\item[$(3)$] The support $|\mathcal{F}(B)|$ contains all lattice points in $\mathbb{Z}^n$.
\end{itemize}

ref_dict.thm:Reading:
\begin{theorem}[{\cite[Theorem~10.6]{Re14}}]\label{thm:Reading}
If $B$ is of finite type, then $|\mathcal{F}(B)|=\mathbb{R}^n$.
\end{theorem}

expanded_sketch:
The post-theorem text only indicates sources/strategies for the implications: it says $(1)\Rightarrow(2)$ is known (\begin{theorem}[{\cite[Theorem~10.6]{Re14}}]\label{thm:Reading}
If $B$ is of finite type, then $|\mathcal{F}(B)|=\mathbb{R}^n$.
\end{theorem}); for skew-symmetric matrices, $(2)\Rightarrow(1)$ was proved in HY25 via categorification using Jacobian algebras; and it states that the present paper gives a complete proof (of the missing implication in general) using scattering diagrams and their pull-back constructions.

