the authors argue as follows. They show that, for an appropriate bipartite graph on a torus, point-line configurations satisfying the conditions (V) and (F) are in bijection with pairs of polygons $P,Q$ such that $Q$ is inscribed in $P$ and $T(Q)$ in $T(P)$. They then provide a sequence of local moves that preserves the combinatorics of the graph but replaces $P,Q$ with $T(P),T(Q)$. Since the combinatorics remains the same and (V),(F) survive under these local moves, it follows that $T(P)$ and $T(Q)$ satisfy the same conditions as $P$ and $Q$; in particular, $T(T(Q))$ is inscribed in $T(T(P))$, i.e. $T^2(Q)$ is inscribed in $T^2(P)$.

